# Detecting SSL Access in PHP to Enforce Secure Connections

Enforcing HTTPS for sensitive endpoints like login pages is a fundamental security practice, but implementing the redirection logic requires careful attention to HTTP headers and server configuration. In PHP, determining whether the current request arrived over an encrypted connection is straightforward using the `$_SERVER` superglobal array. Specifically, you need to inspect the `HTTPS` environment variable or, more reliably, the `REQUEST_SCHEME` in modern PHP versions (7.2+), though checking for a non-empty `HTTPS` value remains the most widely compatible approach.

The standard pattern involves checking if the `$_SERVER['HTTPS']` variable is set and contains a truthy value. When a browser makes an HTTPS request, the web server typically sets this variable to "on". Conversely, for HTTP requests, it often remains unset or empty. You can implement this check immediately at the top of your script, before any output is sent to the client:

```php
<?php
if (!isset($_SERVER['HTTPS']) || $_SERVER['HTTPS'] === 'off') {
    header('Location: https://' . $_SERVER['HTTP_HOST'] . $_SERVER['REQUEST_URI']);
    exit;
}
```

This snippet redirects the user to the HTTPS version of the same URL if the current connection is not secure. Note the use of `exit` after sending the header; failing to stop script execution can lead to partial responses or security issues if subsequent code attempts to modify headers.

### The Importance of HSTS

While the redirect logic handles individual requests, relying solely on client-side redirection has limitations. If a user manually types an HTTP URL or clicks a link pointing to `http://`, they will be redirected to HTTPS, but the initial connection was still unencrypted. This opens a window for man-in-the-middle attacks if the attacker can intercept traffic before the redirect occurs.

To mitigate this, you should consider implementing HTTP Strict Transport Security (HSTS). By sending an `Strict-Transport-Security` header, you instruct the browser to only access your site over HTTPS for a specified duration (defined by the `max-age` attribute). This prevents browsers from attempting to connect via HTTP in the future.

You can add this header alongside your SSL check:

```php
<?php
// Enforce HSTS before any other output
header('Strict-Transport-Security: max-age=31536000; includeSubDomains');

if (!isset($_SERVER['HTTPS']) || $_SERVER['HTTPS'] === 'off') {
    header('Location: https://' . $_SERVER['HTTP_HOST'] . $_SERVER['REQUEST_URI']);
    exit;
}
```

### Configuration Considerations

It is important to distinguish between application-level checks and server-level enforcement. The code above works regardless of the web server software (Apache, Nginx, or others), making it portable across different hosting environments. However, relying entirely on PHP headers can introduce latency if the redirect happens every time a user mistypes a URL.

For production environments, configuring your web server to automatically redirect HTTP traffic to HTTPS at the load balancer or reverse proxy level is often preferable. This offloads the logic from the application and ensures that no plaintext data ever traverses the network between the client and your backend. If you are using Apache, you might configure a virtual host to handle this before the request reaches PHP. If you are on Nginx, you can set up an `if` block or server directive to redirect all non-HTTPS traffic.

### Handling Edge Cases

Be mindful of mixed content scenarios. Even if your login page is served over HTTPS, it might inadvertently load resources from HTTP sources (like images or fonts), which browsers will flag as "Mixed Content." This breaks the security context you are trying to establish. Always ensure that assets loaded within these secure endpoints also originate from `https://`.

Additionally, while `$_SERVER['HTTPS']` is generally reliable, some legacy configurations or specific proxy setups might behave differently. For instance, if a load balancer terminates SSL and forwards requests as HTTP, the upstream server might not see the original scheme unless the `X-Forwarded-Proto` header is present. In such cases, checking for that header becomes necessary:

```php
$ssl = isset($_SERVER['HTTPS']) && $_SERVER['HTTPS'] !== 'off';
if (!$ssl && isset($_SERVER['HTTP_X_FORWARDED_PROTO']) && $_SERVER['HTTP_X_FORWARDED_PROTO'] === 'https') {
    $ssl = true;
}
```

This logic ensures that even in a complex infrastructure where SSL termination happens upstream, your application still recognizes the original secure context and skips unnecessary redirects.
